##
   The `ref<T>` tier executing in the VM, which per AGENTS.md 2.10 is where a
   construct has to work as well as in LLVM.

   The `$` on the callees is what makes this reachable at all: without it
   `dollar_verifier` correctly refuses a non-dollar call from a dollar scope, and
   the bytecode builder erases the function. `$` is `MIRDollarMode::ReadOnly`,
   and a read-only function may still be called from a `Full` (`$$`) scope.

   All three ways of obtaining a reference are exercised, plus a field access
   through one:

     var r: ref<i32> = @n;   annotation
     f(@n)                   argument
     return @n;              return value

   Dereferencing a `ref` needs no `marked` block -- that is the whole value of
   the tier -- but reaching a *field* through it still does, because there is no
   auto-deref.

   Declaration order matters here as everywhere: `P` is declared before the
   functions that name it in a signature.
##
struct P { x: i32, y: i32 }

$ func by_param(r: ref<i32>): i32 { return ^r; }
$ func make(): ref<i32> { var n: i32 = 11i32; return @n; }
$ func struct_field(r: ref<P>): i32 { return marked { ^r.x }; }

$ func foo(): u32 {
  var a = $${
    var n: i32 = 5i32;
    var by_ann: i32 = by_param(@n);
    var m = make();
    var by_ret: i32 = ^m;
    var p: P = .P{.x = 3i32, .y = 4i32};
    var by_field: i32 = struct_field(@p);
    by_ann + by_ret + by_field
  };
  return 0u32;
}
